"She had a fever accompanied by chills.": "dce39caa80a9",
"The four stages run from surface to core. The defense-level stage is the shallowest, corresponding to early symptoms like fever and chills as a pathogen first meets the body's surface defenses. If unresolved, illness is said to progress to the qi-level stage, marked by higher fever without chills, as the pathogen moves past the surface. Beyond that lies the nutritive-level stage, where disturbed sleep and a reddened tongue are said to appear as the pathogen reaches deeper, more vital tissue. The blood-level stage is the deepest and most serious, traditionally associated with bleeding or severe agitation, as the pathogen affects blood itself.": "963fc4fdc21c",
"The illness began to progress more quickly than expected.": "1ed23965fa29",
"The issue remained unresolved for several weeks.": "c9c4e99d3b38",
"The patient showed signs of agitation before the procedure.": "26c67070f207",
"The practical value of the four-layer model is that it turns an otherwise vague question — \"is this getting worse?\" — into a specific one: which of the four layers is a given set of symptoms consistent with, and is the pattern of symptoms moving toward the surface or toward the core. This is one more example of a theme running through the whole course: Chinese medicine consistently prefers naming a specific stage or direction over describing severity in only vague terms like \"mild\" or \"serious.\"": "1f8e769a4eba",
"This course has, across several lessons, treated qi and blood as substances the body produces and relies on. A framework called \"defense, qi, nutritive, blood\" puts those same categories, plus a fourth layer, to a different use: mapping how a warm-type illness is traditionally understood to progress through the body in four increasingly deep stages, rather than describing what qi and blood do when the body is healthy.": "af4a0bb0e762",
